$\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(Δ_{1})f(x, y)dA - ∬_(Δ_{2})f(x, y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135140 1064 "Let's suppose that we want to calculate the double integral\n\n$$\\iint_\\limits{D} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65f622409e3fdd43f56dde79513e8693751465bb131dd828ce9f6cdfaa7d48aa.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ can be represented as the difference of sets $D = \\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 4, -2 \\leq y \\leq 3 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186135143 1064 "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C$, and we select a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135143 1064 "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1,$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186135144 1064 "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, and has a hole.\n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D$. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2\n$$\n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r + \\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2$. For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135144 1064 "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ and has a hole.\n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D.$ So, in this case, we have $$\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2.$$\n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r + \\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2.$ For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186135145 1064 "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D$.\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D$, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135145 1064 "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D.$\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D,$ we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186135146 1064 "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2\n$$\n\nis positively oriented.\n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below\n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{1} &= C_{3}∪C_{8} \\\\\nC_{2} &= C_{5}∪C_{10} \\\\\n∂D_{1} &= C_{3}∪C_{4}∪C_{5}∪C_{6}, \\\\\n∂D_{2} &= C_{8}∪(-C_{6})∪C_{10}∪(-C_{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(∂D)F \\cdot dr &= ∮_(C_{1}∪C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= ∮_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∮_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= \\int_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{8})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{5})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{10})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= + \\int_(C_{4})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(-C_{4})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{6})F \\cdot dr + ∮_(-C_{6})F \\cdot dr_(⏟)_(0) \\\\\n&= \\int_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{4})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{5})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{6})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= + \\int_(C_{8})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(-C_{6})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{10})F \\cdot dr + ∮_(-C_{4})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= ∮_(∂D_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∮_(∂D_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= ∮_(∂D_{1})Pdx + Qdy + ∮_(∂D_{2})Pdx + Qdy \\\\\n&= ∬_(D_{1})(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA + ∬_(D_{2})(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135146 1064 "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral\n\n$$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented.\n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below\n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\ & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186135149 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = QR$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=QR$, can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)x &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q^{T})\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 2; 3x_{2} = 6\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x_{1} - 4(2) &= 2 \\\\\n2x_{1} &= 10 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186135149 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and $$Q = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad R = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{b} = [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$R \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation,