of a constant is just the constant itself. That is, for any constant $a$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a] = a\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[5] = 5,\\qquad \\textrm E[-99] = -99,\\qquad \\textrm E[\\sqrt 2] = \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nThe idea that the expected value of a constant is equal to the same constant makes intuitive sense. If $a$ is a constant, then it does not vary. Therefore, it is always sure to have the same value." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186158494 1064 "There are several important properties that we can use to simplify expressions involving expected values.\n\nThe first property is that the expected value of a constant is just the constant itself. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have $$\\textrm E[a] = a.$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5] = 5,\\qquad \\textrm E[-99] = -99,\\qquad \\textrm E[\\sqrt 2] = \\sqrt 2.$$\n\nThe idea that the expected value of a constant is equal to the same constant makes intuitive sense. If $a$ is a constant, then it does not vary. Therefore, it is always sure to have the same value." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186158495 1064 "We can also factor constants outside of an expected value expression. So for any constant $a$ and any random variable $X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X]\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[5X] = 5 \\textrm E[X],\\qquad\\textrm E[-9X] = -9 \\textrm E[X], \\qquad \\textrm E[\\pi X] = \\pi \\textrm E[X]\n$$\n\nWe can prove this rule using the definition of the expected value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[aX] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)ax \\cdot f(x) \\\\\n&= a∑_(x ∈ S)x \\cdot f(x) \\\\\n&= aE[X]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186158495 1064 "We can also factor constants outside of an expected value expression. So for any constant $a$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5X] = 5 \\textrm E[X],\\qquad\\textrm E[-9X] = -9 \\textrm E[X], \\qquad \\textrm E[\\pi X] = \\pi \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nWe can prove this rule using the definition of the expected value, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[aX] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}ax\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a\\text{E}[X]\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186158552 1064 "Finally, we can combine the factor and constant rules into one simple rule.\n\nSuppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. By combining our two rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX + b] = a\\textrm E[X] + b\n$$\n\nFor example, if we know that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = 4\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[3X + 2] &= E[3X + 2] \\\\\n&= 3E[X] + 2 \\\\\n&= 3(4) + 2 \\\\\n&= 12 + 2 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186158552 1064 "Finally, we can combine the factor and constant rules into one simple rule.\n\nSuppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. By combining our two rules, we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX + b] = a\\textrm E[X] + b.$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+2] & =\\text{E}[3X+2] \\\\ & =3\\text{E}[X]+2 \\\\ & =3(4)+2 \\\\ & =12+2 \\\\ & =14.\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163228 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163228 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$A^T = A$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$\n\nOn the other hand, the matrix\n\n$$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163230 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$. The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163230 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163238 1064 "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A$, and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A$.\n\nFor example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163238 1064 "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix $$A =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$$ has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}_{A} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{U} \\: \\underbrace{\\vphantom{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\\Sigma} \\: \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]^T}_{V^T}$$\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163249 1064 "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,\\ldots, n\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T\n$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1} &= \\frac{v_{1}}{‖v_{1}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu_{2} &= \\frac{v_{2}}{‖v_{2}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= λ_{1}u_{1}u_{1}^{T} + λ_{2}u_{2}u_{2}^{T} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + (-4) \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + (-4) \\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6_(⏟)_(λ_{1}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u_{1}u_{1}^{T}) + (-4)_(⏟)_(λ_{2}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u_{2}u_{2}^{T})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163249 1064 "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$A=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$A =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163252 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A$.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V$.\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n$\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i$,\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V$, respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U$.\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$, let's find the matrices $U$ and $V$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A$:\n$A^{T}A|= \\begin{bmatrix}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma$, we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9$.\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n$u_{1} = \\frac{1}{σ_{1}}Av_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\frac{1}{σ_{2}}Av_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sq