ar vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163253 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and\n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$. Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition.\n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}A &= (UΣV^{T})^{T}(UΣV^{T}) \\\\\n&= (VΣ^{T}U^{T})(UΣV^{T}) \\\\\n&= VΣ^{T}(U^{T}U)ΣV^{T} \\\\\n&= VΣ^{T}(I_{n})ΣV^{T} \\\\\n&= V(Σ^{T}Σ)V^{T}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left(\\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A$. So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A$.\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A$, we obtain\n$A^{T}A|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Solving the characteristic equation $\\mid A^T \\! A - \\lambda I \\mid = 0$, we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$:\n${\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}$\nSo, the corresponding singular values are\n${\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} = 4}, \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}$.\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A$. These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I)$, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I)$, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163253 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and\n\n$$A= U\\Sigma V^T$$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition.\n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\ & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is