 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].$$ Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get $${\\color{red} [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}.$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$ $$\\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad V = [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163254 1064 "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix, and\n\n![](images/ec/ec033e8518a97ff363494df7effa3c6f032b7cfaa028712b87959d659ea12962.svg) is a singular value decomposition of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Let's now describe how to compute the matrix ![](images/c3/c3ae10e66e0dee45c359329a1cd8b0246634f95fdce2f5bf206d04dac3594fc5.svg) of the SVD using the matrices ![](images/12/128e4b474ccbb86ef9b1a105a348e493595f4084d05e9cfa5f7889d390d5eac4.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d936a1af541b15fe85bff8b60fbe1d8d1851f9e91616cf4799dd0c5323d038a.svg)\n\nRecall that ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) is orthogonal, meaning that ![](images/c7/c71eaab408a38ca1a1d0c653fe51ab02287d8ffbe4ef0461c93186579552d456.svg) Consequently, multiplying ![](images/c9/c99b0facd0ff831056deed9aaa5356ca5cbeed72ed9f632604cd5fb9479c3b7a.svg) by ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) on the right, we obtain\n\n![](images/03/036ae766d4187370271793b6bbacc9841b444173ff031740acbd259531ed5126.svg)\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b9/b924929217b058d3edefff9a6557d3ad8b5d6b3536e7ac0dcaf185e50b60bb5a.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/64/64b1897c8532aee8fee0d4f76f632cc91c6a5a4078b11bfaaea07235aeadcc3f.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/fe/fedbe16eb8c09802bf435c256bb225249c1ee578dd558d0f3b6b284a56236517.svg) Finally, provided that ![](images/66/668ce05b1a81d3d120c47a7dadea446891ef986edb5f499c592f310755bb143c.svg) we obtain\n\n![](images/ec/ecd9fe71eaef2172512d70bfb78ffea6e36b973e6f96c09f0480ef2e0e9e55ad.svg)\n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9d412fae57d02f4fc44b04a277e1a6512e3f560e3152416a69d30d72e38a9ac.svg) Let's see an example." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163254 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $2\\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$. Let's now describe how to compute the matrix $U$ of the SVD using the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$.\n\nRecall that $V$ is orthogonal, meaning that\n\n$$\nV^T \\! V = I\n$$\n\nConsequently, multiplying\n\n$$\nA = U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nby $V$ on the right, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= UΣV^{T} \\\\\nAV &= UΣ(V^{T}V) \\\\\nAV &= UΣ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= v_{1} \\mid v_{2} \\\\\n\\mid \\mid \\mid ] &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= u_{1} \\mid u_{2} \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & σ_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= Av_{1} \\mid Av_{2} \\\\\n\\mid \\mid \\mid ] &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= σ_{1}u_{1} \\mid σ_{2}u_{2} \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}_i = \\sigma_i \\mathbf{u}_i\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,2$. Finally, provided that\n\n$$\n\\sigma_i \\ne 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix $\\mathbf u$. Let's see an example." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163260 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163260 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if $$A = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ then the product $A\\cdot B$ is $$\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$ which is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163279 1064 "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions.\n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163279 1064 "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions.\n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163280 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[3X] &= 3^{2} \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[- 4X] &= (-4)^{2} \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[aX] &= E[(aX - E[aX])^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[(aX - aE[X])^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[a^{2}(X - E[X])^{2}] \\\\\n&= a^{2}E[(X - E[X])^{2}] \\\\\n&= a^{2}Var[X]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186163280 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =18.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =32.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186163281 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b$:\n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other.\n\nFor example, if we know that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[2X + 1] &= 2^{2} \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194